# The three fixed Hindi cases, and what they hashed to.
#
# These are from before lang/hien's alphabet held Devanagari, when every
# Devanagari code point arrived as its low byte and a word came out as a row of
# punctuation. They are kept rather than re-recorded because the state after
# the code point table is worse to freeze: the letters arrive correctly now and
# no rule knows what any of them sounds like, so all three cases fall to the
# same default and hash identically. test/hien-bless.sh refuses to record that,
# deliberately.
#
# So test/hien-hash.sh fails today and is meant to. What it is waiting for is
# the letter-to-sound rules; test/hien-arrives.py is the check that says in one
# line where the work has got to, and it is the one to run in the meantime.
826a8bbfaf475a0af6f8396ea29916a9fbf974db37420e76e9b8ce3240698aa3  plain
fd800e64c506ba409a366485f76479adc97b04b24a5e714cf9312538ea2dacab  matra
d08217e0826e7164947dd7835760c9a78f47a669894122eef9d322f4cc04645e  utf8
